# Architecture layers

```text
src/
├── domain/           # Engine-agnostic contracts (book, document, sync location)
├── application/      # Use cases + Zustand stores
├── features/         # React UI only
├── infrastructure/   # PDF.js, IndexedDB, HTTP auth/sync, adapters
├── app/              # Shell, routing, global styles
└── config/           # Feature flags, API base URL
```

## Dependency rules

- `features/` may call `application/` and read `domain/` types — never IndexedDB, PDF.js, or `fetch` to the API directly.
- `application/` coordinates use cases; persistence and network go through `infrastructure/` interfaces.
- `domain/` has no React, no IndexedDB, no HTTP.
- Document engines live behind adapters (`infrastructure/document-engine/`). Sources behind `document-source` / `book-source`.
- Components must justify new layers — no ceremony-only abstractions.

## Identity and state

- Logical book identity is content-based (SHA-256), not filename/path.
- Reading state is per-device; UI must not assume a single global cursor.
- Cloud features gated by `features.cloudEnabled` / `VITE_CLOUD_ENABLED` (default off).

## ❌ / ✅

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — UI talking to storage or PDF.js
await idb.put('books', book);
await pdfjs.getDocument(data);

// ✅ GOOD — UI → application store / use case
await libraryStore.importBook(file);
await readerStore.openBook(bookId);
```
